WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the Ampropenguin.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myths and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The curiosities I find most fascinating of all.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
The Ampropenguin

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Some animals have mastered the art of deception.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
The cuckoo tricks other birds into raising its young.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.000
While the death's head hawk moth infiltrates the nests of bees to steal their precious honey.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
They're cheats and imposters.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The call of the cuckoo has long been regarded as a sign of spring.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
But in fact, it's the call of a killer and a cheat.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:41.000
The cuckoo lays its egg in the nests of other birds and somehow persuades them to treat it and its chick as if it were their own.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.000
How does it get away with it? It's a question that has puzzled people for centuries.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.000
In Britain, the cuckoo arrives at a time when most birds are nesting and laying eggs.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Early egg collectors noticed that the nests of some birds had a slightly odd looking egg in them.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
These are the eggs laid by a number of different birds.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Marsh warbler, spotted flycatcher, linnet and the white throat.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.000
And amongst each of those clutches there is a fraudster, a cuckoo egg, which mimics that of its host.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Although cuckoos were long known to lay their eggs in the nests of other birds, no one had actually described it happening.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:37.000
And then in the 18th century, an English country doctor with an interest in natural history decided to investigate.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Edward Jenner lived here in Barkley Gloucestershire and is best known for his work on the smallpox vaccine.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:56.000
In fact, he's said to be the father of vaccination and that his work has saved more human lives than that of any other man.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:05.000
What is less known is that he first achieved scientific distinction by his observations on the behaviour of the cuckoo.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
At the time, it was believed that a cuckoo removes all of the eggs in a nest and then lays its own.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
By doing so, it would ensure its own chick gets all the food brought in by the unwitting nest owners.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
But Edward Jenner's detailed observations were to reveal a rather darker tale.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Jenner's work on cuckoos was published in 1788 here in the Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society, the world's first scientific society.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:50.000
It was entitled simply, Observations on the Natural History of the Cuckoo, by Mr Edward Jenner.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:00.000
In it, he reported that it was not the parent cuckoo, but the newly hatched chick which pushes the eggs and nestlings of the foster parents out of the nest.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
As soon as it hatches, the cuckoo chick's instinct is to kill anything else in the nest.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
It's still blind and naked, but it has a cup-shaped depression on its back into which an egg fits perfectly.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:27.000
But sometimes the other eggs hatch earlier, and Jenner's observations of how the cuckoo chick deals with its nestmates were quite shocking.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:39.000
He writes, the mode of accomplishing this was very curious. The little animal, with the assistance of its rump and wings, contrived to get the bird on its back,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:47.000
and making a lodgment of the bird and by elevating its elbows, clambered backwards with it up the side of the nest till it reached the top,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:55.000
where, resting for a moment, it threw off its load with a jerk and quite disengaged it from the nest.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
The real villain had been uncovered.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Jenner's views were met with incredulity and some disbelief, but nonetheless they earned him the fellowship of the Royal Society.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
It was the greatest honour that could be given to a scientist at the time.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Jenner's observations had revealed the true nature of the cuckoo's deception,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:25.000
but it still wasn't clear why the cuckoo should opt for this strange way of raising its young.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:35.000
It wasn't until a hundred years later that Charles Darwin finally provided an explanation with his theory of evolution.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.000
The cuckoo's behaviour has evolved to increase its own breeding success.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:50.000
By avoiding the task of raising chicks, the cuckoo can lay more eggs than any other bird, as many as 25 in a season.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:59.000
While it makes evolutionary sense for the cuckoo to lay its eggs in the nests of others, what about its victims?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.000
Why do they put up with this trickery?

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.000
It seems that they sometimes don't.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.000
And this was revealed in an early natural history film in 1920.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:18.000
The cuckoo's secret was made by Edgar Chance and Oliver Pike, an egg collector and a wildlife filmmaker.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Chance was fascinated by cuckoos and spent a great deal of time following them.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.000
He was the first person known to see a cuckoo lay its egg.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.000
The deception involves stealth and speed.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.000
The female waits until a nest is unattended and then she strikes.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.000
But if she's spotted, the owners fight back.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.000
If she's successful, the whole deception takes less than 10 seconds.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:52.000
She removes and eats just one egg and replaces it with her own.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.000
The Chance and Pike film solved one mystery, but there were still others.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.000
How does the cuckoo choose its victim?

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
And why don't the nest owners reject the alien egg?

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Read warblers are one of the cuckoo's main targets and the pair has a nest just in here.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:25.000
The female warbler has laid four speckled eggs and using them to lay eggs, the cuckoo is able to eat the egg.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.000
The cuckoo is able to eat the egg.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.000
The cuckoo is able to eat the egg.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.000
The cuckoo is able to eat the egg.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:39.000
The female warbler has laid four speckled eggs and using a mottled egg, I can illustrate the cuckoo's trickery.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.000
This is the sort of egg that the cuckoo would lay in the reed warbler's nest.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:53.000
It matches the reed warbler's actual eggs very closely in color.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Experiments with mottled eggs have shown that reed warblers have become very good at recognizing an alien egg and either throw it out or desert their nest to start afresh.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.000
So the cuckoo has to make sure that it produces an egg that is a very good match.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.000
The cuckoo and its victims are evolving competitively.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:27.000
With each generation, cuckoos improve their mimicry, while the nest owners become better at spotting a foreign egg.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:39.000
While many birds are very good at detecting a strange egg in their nest, they seem incapable of recognizing the monstrous cuckoo chick as an imposter.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.000
But the deception is not complete.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:50.000
The young cuckoo is much larger than the reed warbler chick, so it also needs a lot more food.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.000
How does it get enough?

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.000
The cuckoo has a solution.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:03.000
It now uses vocal deception to trick its foster parents into providing more food.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:10.000
This is the sonogram of the sound waves produced by a single reed warbler chick begging for food.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:16.000
And below it is the call of a cuckoo chick, and as you can see, it looks very different.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:24.000
In fact, it more closely resembles the calls of a whole nestful of reed warbler chicks.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:34.000
So the cuckoo chick's call is a super stimulus that sounds like a whole nestful of chicks.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.000
And it appears to work.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:47.000
The adult birds rush back and forth, providing the imposter with the same amount of food as they would for an entire brood of their own.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:10:00.000
At three weeks old, the cuckoo chick has spilled out of the nest. It's now almost eight times the size of its foster parent.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:09.000
It was over 200 years ago that Edward Jenner first shocked us with his revelation of the cuckoo's extraordinary lifestyle.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:22.000
Now we know that its unusual behavior is due to an extraordinary arms race that has resulted in one of the most fascinating specializations in nature.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:30.000
In part two, we meet another imposter that specializes in stealing from the nests of honeybees.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:42.000
Warbler nests are not an easy target for invading cuckoos, but their success relies on deceiving just two parent birds.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:49.000
The Death's Head Hound is a very dangerous species, and it's a very dangerous species.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.000
It can be a threat to two parent birds.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:58.000
The Death's Head Hawk Moth is an imposter too, but it deceives hundreds of bees.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:04.000
How does this eerie moth fly into bees' nests and live to tell the tale?

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:10.000
This wonderful creature was once one of the most feared insects in Europe.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:15.000
It's a Death's Head Hawk Moth, and it's easy enough to see how it got its name.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.000
It has a mark on its back that looks just like a human skull.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:33.000
This gave it a bad reputation that lasted for centuries, but now there are new ideas about this moth's strange appearance that may help explain its extraordinary ability to rob hives without being stung.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:43.000
Death's Head Hawk Moths are a rare sight in Britain, for they spend most of their lives in Africa and Asia.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:51.000
But every summer, a small number of migrants arrive in Northern Europe, and if the weather is warm enough, they breed.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:59.000
Their caterpillars, unlike the drab adult moths, are beautifully coloured.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.000
After feeding for several weeks, they can grow to a length of 13 centimetres.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Once ready to become adults, they pupate in the soil and emerge as the sinister, strange, Dupatin moths.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:30.000
In the early 19th century, a region of northern France was hit by a terrible pestilence, and at the same time a large number of hawk moths were seen in the area.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:38.000
The local people linked the deaths to these night-flying insects, but there was another, even more disturbing side to this moth.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.000
It could make an unusual noise.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:50.000
There, a strange squeak, and that only added to its chilling reputation.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.000
Moths don't usually squeak.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:07.000
Tiger moths sometimes produce ultrasonic warning clicks that tell bats that they're poisonous and not good to eat, but this is not a noise we can generally hear.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:13.000
Perhaps the deathshead hawk moth squeaks to scare predators like birds.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:19.000
However, other large migratory moths don't make such a sound.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:26.000
This makes the deathshead hawk moth squeak all the more surprising, and it has intrigued people for centuries.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.000
These moths are more than 200 years old.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:43.000
We know that because the handwritten label there tells us they were connected in 1801 by Robert Darling Willis, the personal physician to King George the Third.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:57.000
George the Third is well known as the king who suffered from bouts of madness, and on a visit to see the king during one of them, Dr. Willis discovered these large moths in the monarch's bedchamber.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:05.000
Unable to identify them, the doctor sent them to his grandson, who was at that time superintendent of the Museum of Zoology in Cambridge.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:19.000
He confirmed that they were deathshead hawk moths, and unusually for an insect, this moth produces a loud call that has been likened to the mournful cry of a grief-stricken child.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:25.000
Did the disturbed king hear the plaintive cause of a hawk moth? That we don't know.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:35.000
But certainly many of the ordinary people of the 19th century were struck with a sense of terror whenever this moth appeared.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:42.000
The moth's unusual appearance and strange behaviour baffle people.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.000
But in nature, such traits usually have a purpose.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:54.000
And it may be for the deathshead hawk moth that they enable it to break into beehives and steal their honey.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:03.000
These are the giant honey bees of South East Asia, and they form some of the largest bee colonies in the world.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:10.000
I once got up close to one in order to demonstrate their response to a predator.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:18.000
I had a model of a large hornet, which produced a kind of Mexican wave, and that makes it very difficult for an aggressor to land.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:24.000
This covering of bees looks impossible to penetrate.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:31.000
But at night, a thief can break through their ranks.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:39.000
A deathshead hawk moth lands on the carpet of bees and pushes its way through without being attacked.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.000
In just a few seconds, it takes some sips of honey and emerges unharmed.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:51.000
Getting past the guard bees is quite a feat, but surviving inside is even more astounding.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:56.000
Deathshead hawk moths raid domestic beehives too, and can be quite a pest.

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:11.000
Somehow, the moth slips past the guards and, as if invisible, walks through the hive, heading straight for the honeycomb.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:20.000
It then feeds unnoticed. How does it do this?

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:27.000
One theory proposes that its spooky appearance may help it avoid being attacked.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:35.000
Miriam Rothschild, a great entomologist and expert in the field of honey, is a very good beekeeper.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:41.000
To support her, she does a lot of research, and she has the best information.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.000
But what if the moth is attacked?

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:51.000
Miriam Rothschild, a great entomologist and expert on fleas and butterflies,

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:59.000
suggested that the moth's skull pattern looks like the head of a worker bee, and that this could play a role in the moth's deception.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.000
close-up photo of the skull pattern of the moth.

00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:06.120
There.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:09.160
Well, I suppose there's a slight resemblance,

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:12.560
but given the fact that most moths raid bee hives and nests

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:15.200
during the night, it's unlikely the bees

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:17.520
could see that much detail.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:21.680
The most likely answer lies in the scent the moth gives off.

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:26.160
In America in the 1950s,

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.240
a German entomologist called Thomas Eisner

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:31.360
studied chemical ecology,

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:34.520
in particular, the chemical defences of insects.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:40.880
Most famously, he illustrated how bombardier beetles

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.400
fire hot acid onto a predator.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:51.080
He also studied moths and showed that the feathery projections

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:53.640
on their abdomens and their large antennae

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:57.240
were used to produce and pick up scent.

00:17:57.240 --> 00:18:01.480
It seemed that many insects were using scent in surprising ways.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.200
Tests on the chemical scents produced by hawkmoths

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.840
reveal a remarkable similarity to those produced by the worker bees

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.040
in the hives that they raid.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:13.880
Their scent is not identical,

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:15.760
but it contains several key chemicals

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.200
that exactly match those produced by bees.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:20.720
So, the Death's Head hawkmoth's scent

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.240
acts as an invisibility cloak

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:26.600
that makes it undetectable to the worker bees in the nest.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:35.480
With thick scales on its body, clawed feet that grip the honeycomb,

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:38.960
and a short pointed proboscis to pierce the honey cells,

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:42.400
the moth has evolved into an effective hive robber.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:45.880
But there is another, even more impressive imposter

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:50.320
that can also penetrate the protective defences of an insect colony.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.680
Its victims are not bees, but ants.

00:18:55.760 --> 00:19:01.000
The imposter that invades this ant nest doesn't get in there by flying.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.080
Nothing as blatant as that.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:06.040
Instead, the caterpillars of some species

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:08.280
are able to get in there by flying.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:12.560
Nothing as blatant as that. Instead, the caterpillars of some species

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:14.880
of blue butterfly, like this one,

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:17.880
wait for red ants to collect them.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:20.880
Remarkably, passing ants don't kill them.

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:24.280
They pick them up and take them back into their nest.

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:29.880
The cuckoo caterpillar will stay inside the nest for up to ten months.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.280
Just like the Death's Head hawkmoth,

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.880
it produces a chemical scent that deceives the ants.

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:40.480
This pink caterpillar, which belongs to the Alcon blue butterfly,

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:43.080
has been collected because, to them,

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.280
it smells just like the young of their own nest.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:50.280
They become controlled by the imposter's intoxicating scent

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:54.680
and feed the butterfly larva even more regularly than they do their own.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:00.880
There's another way this imposter pulls off its deceptive trick.

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:02.680
When it's inside the nest,

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:06.280
the butterfly larva makes a strange chattering noise.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:10.080
To our ears, it's very faint, but it's clear enough to other insects.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.080
This is it.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:19.080
And this is the sound that's made by a queen ant.

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.080
To worker ants, these calls are very similar,

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:32.080
and they react by treating the butterfly larva

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:34.080
as if it's one of their own.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:40.080
Caterpillars of the blue butterfly are impressive imposters.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.080
Not only do they mimic the scent of the ants,

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:45.080
but they're queens' calls, too.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:51.080
This seems to trump the Death's Head hawkmoth's ability as a nest invader.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:59.080
But the hawkmoth may also be using sound to trick its victims.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:05.080
Remember the eerie squeak that was thought to be so frightening?

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:10.080
There.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.080
The hawkmoth makes this sound inside the beehive

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:15.080
when it enters to steal honey.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:18.080
It's been suggested that this might calm the bees

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:21.080
because the squeak is thought to sound like the piping call

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:25.080
that the queen honeybee makes to pacify her workers.

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.080
We can't be sure if the call and the strange skull marking

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.080
evolved to deceive bees,

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:36.080
but we can be certain that the Death's Head hawkmoth's life as an imposter

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:39.080
is more curious than the superstitions

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:42.080
that have surrounded it for hundreds of years.

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:46.080
The cuckoo and the hawkmoth are both audacious imposters,

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:50.080
but the cuckoo's ability to make its victim raise its young

00:21:50.080 --> 00:22:13.080
is perhaps the most accomplished deception of all.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:22.080
Thank you for watching!
